Agreed on points earlier vs. later. As I stated above there are costs in
acquiring protection AC both at lower and higher XLs. My balance point
is that when done earlier you AC needs are not as rigid simply because
early monsters in the upper dungeon are not as much of an issue. There's
a lot of difference between a jackal or a goblin and ants, killer bees,
and mumaks. Even a pack of rothes can be deadly without proper protection.

Armor influences a lot of variables incluing AC, weight, and spellcasting
failure, along with magic cancellation.  Each has tradeoffs. Protection AC on
the other hand only influences AC.  So it facilitates being able to make
different choices about armor that affects the categories above. I'm sure my
bias is because I typically play spellcasting classes (wizard, healer, monk).
So I see the impact of putting on mithril for example on spellcasting. With
protection AC I can make different choices and still have the AC I need to
survive (studded leather for example) and to have spellcasting flexibility.
With monks it's particularly bad because wearing armor completely kills
to hit ability.

The only thing about AC that's straightforward in this game is the fact that
you have to have it. Everything else is a puzzle, trying to balance a host
of factors. That's what makes the game interesting, to a point.

The point is when a character finally gets ?oEA and holy water. Then AC
points comes in droves, with the same attributes as protection points.
From then on in a game, AC is pretty much moot as an issue. But characters
have to live long enough to get there.

